<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hamengkubuwono IX</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hamengkubuwono IX (1912–1988) was sultan of Yogyakarta from 1940 until his death and a leading figure of the Indonesian independence struggle. He supported the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> from 1945, offered Yogyakarta as its capital from 1946 to 1949 and became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/special-region-of-yogyakarta">Special Region of Yogyakarta</a>. He held senior ministerial posts and served as vice-president of Indonesia from 1973 to 1978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hamengkubuwono-ix.jpg" alt="Hamengkubuwono IX" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Departemen Penerangan Republik Indonesia (1973) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hamengku_Buwono_IX_(1973).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hamengkubuwono IX was born on 12 April 1912 in Yogyakarta as Gusti Raden Mas Dorodjatun, the ninth son of Prince Gusti Pangeran Puruboyo, later Hamengkubuwono VIII. At the age of four he was sent to live with the Mulder family, who were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and called him Henkie. He attended Dutch schools in Yogyakarta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bandung">Bandung</a>, and in 1930 went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where he finished the Lyceum Haarlem in 1934 and then studied Indology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>. He returned to the Indies in October 1939 without finishing, because of the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. His father died on 22 October 1939, and his relatives agreed that he should succeed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was crowned on 18 March 1940, after four months of negotiation with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> governor, Lucien Adam, over the terms of the political contract. In his coronation speech he said that although he had tasted Western education, he was and would always be a Javanese. Under the Japanese occupation, which began in 1942, he kept autonomy in his area, and he is credited with sparing many of his people forced labour by manipulating agricultural statistics and with proposing the irrigation canal between the Progo and Opak rivers.</p><p>After the declaration of independence on 17 August 1945, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paku-alam-viii">Paku Alam VIII</a> decided to support the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, which made him governor of Yogyakarta, now a Special Region. In early 1946 the capital was quietly moved to Yogyakarta, and he gave the new government funds. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> troops occupied the city on 21 December 1948 and arrested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and Hatta, he stayed and refused to cooperate with Dutch plans to make him head of a federal state.</p><p>He conceived the General Offensive of 1 March 1949, in which Lieutenant Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s troops held Yogyakarta for six hours, with his palace used as a hideout; Suharto later claimed credit for the idea in his 1989 autobiography. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> handed authority over Yogyakarta to him on 30 June 1949. He then served as minister of defence and in other posts, and as minister of economics, finance and industry from 1966 to 1973. In March 1978, after troops took over the Bandung Technological Institute when its students published a book against Suharto's re-election, he declined renomination as vice-president, citing his health.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He led the national Scout movement from 1968 and received the Bronze Wolf of the World Organization of the Scout Movement in 1973. He died on 2 October 1988 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, after sudden internal bleeding, and was buried in the royal mausoleum of the Mataram monarchs at Imogiri. A museum in the palace at Yogyakarta is dedicated to him. He was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a>, and his son succeeded him as Hamengkubuwono X.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hamengkubuwono IX” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan <b>Hamengkubuwono IX</b> sided with the Republic of Indonesia, which made Yogyakarta its capital from 1946 to 1949.</blockquote><a href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yogyakarta, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Yogyakarta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hamengkubuwono IX"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4190 5665h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4190 5665h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-7.8,110.39&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
